---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Compare — Tracepod"
  description="How Tracepod's runtime-observed image minimization relates to static image minimizers, base-image swaps, and runtime detection tools."
>
  <section class="compare-hero section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="eyebrow">Compare</div>
      <h1 class="heading-xl">Where Tracepod fits.</h1>
      <p class="body-lg" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 680px;">
        A few categories of tools get mentioned in the same breath as Tracepod. Most of
        them aren&rsquo;t competitors so much as adjacent approaches to a related problem &mdash;
        here&rsquo;s how each one actually differs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="compare-lede card">
        <p class="body-md">
          Tracepod's distinguishing choice is <strong>how it decides what belongs in the
          image</strong>: it observes the real workload running in your cluster via eBPF
          and builds the minimized image from what that workload actually opened, rather
          than from a static analysis of the source image or a hand-maintained base. That
          single choice is what the comparisons below come back to.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="compare-grid">

        <div class="compare-card card">
          <div class="eyebrow">Closest comparison</div>
          <h2 class="heading-md">SlimToolkit</h2>
          <p class="body-md">
            SlimToolkit (formerly DockerSlim) is the closest thing to a direct comparison:
            it also produces a minimized image from an existing one. The difference is in
            how each tool decides what to keep. SlimToolkit instruments a container run and
            captures that run's behavior using its own sensor technique; Tracepod uses an
            eBPF kprobe on <code>openat()</code> running as a Kubernetes DaemonSet, profiling
            the workload where it actually runs &mdash; in your cluster, under real traffic,
            for as long as you choose &mdash; and it carries an explicit per-file observation
            source (direct, inferred, manually added) through to a confidence score, so you
            can see exactly how complete a given profiling run was.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="compare-card card">
          <div class="eyebrow">Complementary, not competing</div>
          <h2 class="heading-md">Distroless, Chainguard, Docker Hardened Images</h2>
          <p class="body-md">
            These are base-image swaps: you change your <code>FROM</code> line to a
            smaller, more carefully maintained base and get a reduced surface and
            (depending on the project) a faster CVE-patch cadence for the packages that
            base ships. That's a different axis from what Tracepod does. Tracepod doesn't
            care what your base image is &mdash; it builds a minimized image from
            <em>observed runtime behavior</em> on top of whichever source image you give it.
            The two approaches stack: start from a hardened base, then let Tracepod trim
            it further to exactly what your workload uses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="compare-card card">
          <div class="eyebrow">Different problem</div>
          <h2 class="heading-md">Falco, Tetragon</h2>
          <p class="body-md">
            Falco and Tetragon are runtime detection tools: they watch for anomalous
            or policy-violating behavior <em>while your workload is running in
            production</em>, and alert or block on it. Tracepod also uses eBPF, but for a
            different purpose and at a different point in the lifecycle &mdash; it profiles a
            workload to learn what it needs, then <strong>builds a new, smaller image</strong>
            from that observation. Tracepod doesn't do runtime policy enforcement, and
            Falco/Tetragon don't produce a minimized image. Many teams use detection
            tooling and image minimization together, at different stages of the pipeline.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="compare-card card">
          <div class="eyebrow">Adjacent use case</div>
          <h2 class="heading-md">Kubescape</h2>
          <p class="body-md">
            Kubescape's relevance scoring uses runtime file-activity data to help you
            triage which CVEs in a scan result actually matter &mdash; it tells you which
            vulnerable packages were exercised at runtime, so you can prioritize those
            first. That's a triage layer on top of your existing image; it doesn't change
            what ships in the image itself. Tracepod's reachability reporting answers a
            related question (was the vulnerable package loaded during profiling?), but
            Tracepod's primary output is the minimized image itself, built from that same
            runtime-observation data &mdash; not just a reordered vulnerability list.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="compare-note card">
        <p class="body-md">
          We've tried to describe each of these fairly, based on how their own
          documentation describes them. If something here is out of date or you think
          we've mischaracterized a tool, <a href="/contact">tell us</a> and we'll fix it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band section">
    <div class="container cta-band-inner">
      <div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg">See it against a real image.</h2>
        <p class="body-md" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
          Try the open-source toolchain against one of your own workloads.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-shrink: 0;">
        <a href="/open-source" class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg">Open Source</a>
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Contact us</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
.compare-hero { padding-top: var(--space-16); }

.compare-lede { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; }
.compare-lede strong { color: var(--text-primary); }

.compare-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  gap: var(--space-6);
}
.compare-card h2 { margin-top: var(--space-2); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
.compare-card p { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7; }
.compare-card code {
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: 0.85em;
}

.compare-note { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; }
.compare-note p { color: var(--text-secondary); }

.cta-band { background: var(--bg-elevated); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
.cta-band-inner { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--space-8); flex-wrap: wrap; }
.btn-lg { padding: 0.8rem 1.75rem; font-size: 1rem; }

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .compare-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}
</style>
